        <p>
          Well, you are going to find out how either way.
        </p>
      </header>

      <nav class="topic-toc" aria-label="Page sections">
        <a href="#broad-stroke">Broad Stroke</a>
        <a href="#main-idea">Main Idea</a>
        <a href="#a-deeper-look">A Deeper Look</a>
      </nav>

      <section class="topic-section" id="broad-stroke">
        <h2>Broad Stroke</h2>

        <p>
	  Curve parameterization is a neat and creative way to repackage the information describing a shape in an arbitrary dimension. Take the dry, vanilla
	  approach to formalizing a 2D curve, namely a parabola in this case:
        </p>

        <Equation
          tex={String.raw`f : \R \rightarrow \R, f(x) = x^2.`}
        />

	<p> Every point lying on this curve can be seen as having coordinates </p>

	<Equation
          tex={String.raw`(t, t^2),`}
        />

	<p>
	  where <Math tex = "t"/> is a real variable. Or should we say... <span class="emph">parameter</span>? (*wink* *wink*) This is the most
	  straightforward way to express a parabola in parametric form. The key fact about parameterizations is that they are not unique. The parametric
	  forms listed below still describe the same parabola:
	</p>

	<Equation
          tex={String.raw`(2t, 4t^2),`}
        />

	<Equation
          tex={String.raw`\left(\frac{t}{2}, \frac{t^2}{4}\right),`}
        />

	<Equation
          tex={String.raw`(3\sqrt{3}t, 27t^2),`}
        />

	<Equation
          tex={String.raw`\left( \frac{t - 7}{5}, \frac{t^2 - 14t + 49}{25} \right),`}
        />

	<Equation
          tex={String.raw`(t^3, t^6).`}
        />

	<p> Can you see why that is? The image of all of these is the set of points in the 2D plane: </p>

	<Equation
          tex={String.raw`\{ (x, y) \in \mathbb{R}^2 : y = x^2 \}.`}
        />

	<p> By replacing <Math tex = "x"/> with <Math tex = "2t"/> in the expression <Math tex = "y = x^2"/>, indeed we obtain <Math tex = "y = 4t^2"/>,
	which yields the first reparameterization, <Math tex = "(2t, 4t^2)"/>. We basically introduced a variable <Math tex = "t"/> defined
	as <Math tex = "t:= \frac{x}{2}"/> and rewrote the entire thing in terms of <Math tex = "t"/>. For the other ones, we used </p>

	<Equation
          tex={String.raw`t := 2x \Rightarrow x = \frac{t}{2}, y = \frac{t^2}{4},`}
        />

	<Equation
          tex={String.raw`t := \frac{x\sqrt{3}}{9} \Rightarrow x = 3\sqrt{3}t, y = 27t^2,`}
        />

	<Equation
          tex={String.raw`t := 5x + 7 \Rightarrow x = \frac{t - 7}{5}, y = \frac{t^2 - 14t + 49}{25},`}
        />

	<Equation
          tex={String.raw`t := \sqrt[3]{x} \Rightarrow x = t^3, y = t^6.`}
        />

	<p>
	  If you've been following along up to this point, you might've noticed that we are sort of implicitly defining some kind of itermediate functions
          for which we then find their inverses. Consider specifically the parameterization:
	</p>

	<Equation
          tex={String.raw`\left( \frac{t - 7}{5}, \frac{t^2 - 14t + 49}{25} \right).`}
        />

	We can define the function

	<Equation
          tex={String.raw`\phi : \R \rightarrow \R, \phi(x) = 5x + 7.`}
        />

	<p>
	  Since <Math tex = "\phi"/> is <span class="emph">bijective</span>, aka <span class="emph">invertible</span>, we know that, for
	  every <Math tex = "t := \phi(x)"/> out there, there's a corresponding <Math tex = "x"/>, meaning every real number <Math tex = "x"/> can be rewritten as
	</p>

	<Equation
          tex={String.raw`x = \frac{t - 7}{5},`}
        />

	<p>
	  where <Math tex = "t"/> is another real number. This principle is precisely what allows us to do these reparameterizations in the first place,
	knowing that they will indeed work out and yeild the exact same set of coordinates as the original parameterization. The coordinates of the
        original parametric form, <Math tex = "(t, t^2)"/> can be thought of as functions, <Math tex = "(\alpha_1(t), \alpha_2(t))"/>,
	  where
	</p>

	<Equation
          tex={String.raw`\alpha_1 : \R \rightarrow \R, \alpha_1(t) = t,`}
        />

	<Equation
          tex={String.raw`\alpha_1 : \R \rightarrow \R, \alpha_2(t) = t^2,`}
        />

	<p>
	  and if you think about what the change of parameter does, it is simply computing the values of <Math tex = "\alpha_1"/> and <Math tex = "\alpha_2"/> for
	  our new way of expressing the <Math tex = "x"/> coordinate as <Math tex = "\frac{t - 7}{5}"/>:
	</p>

	<Equation
          tex={String.raw`\alpha_1\left( \frac{t - 7}{5} \right) = \frac{t - 7}{5},`}
        />

	<Equation
          tex={String.raw`\alpha_2\left( \frac{t - 7}{5} \right) = \frac{(t - 7)^2}{5^2} = \frac{t^2 - 14t + 49}{25}.`}
        />

	<p> This specific example is
	what's known as an <span class="emph">affine change of parameter</span>. To generalize, consider a function </p>

	<Equation
          tex={String.raw`\phi : \R \rightarrow \R, \phi(x) = ax + b, a,b \in \R, a \neq 0,`}
        />

	<p> and its inverse, </p>

	<Equation
          tex={String.raw`\phi^{-1}(t) = \frac{t - b}{a}.`}
        />

	<p> The associated change of parameter is then going to be </p>

	<Equation
          tex={String.raw`\left( \frac{t - b}{a}, \left(\frac{t - b}{a}\right)^2 \right).`}
        />

	<p>
	  We can be more adventurous if we want to. Notice what we have to do in order to come up with these changes of parameter is find a bijective
	  function <Math tex = "\phi"/> whose inverse allows us to recover the entire original domain, which for the parabola example is <Math tex = "\mathbb{R}"/>.
	  So clearly <Math tex = "\phi"/> must be defined on <Math tex = "\mathbb{R}"/>, but that doesn't mean its range has to be <Math tex = "\mathbb{R}"/> as well.
	  Just think of any functioned define on <Math tex = "\mathbb{R}"/> whose image is smaller than <Math tex = "\mathbb{R}"/>. For example,
	</p>

	<Equation
          tex={String.raw`\phi : \R \rightarrow \left( -\frac{\pi}{2}, \frac{\pi}{2} \right), \phi(x) = \arctan x.`}
        />

	<p> Its inverse is of course </p>

	<Equation
          tex={String.raw`\phi^{-1}(t) = \tan t,`}
        />

	<p> and we know that the image of the tangent function is the entire real line. So another valid parametric form for a parabola is </p>

	<Equation
          tex={String.raw`(\tan t, \tan^2 t).`}
        />

	<p>
	  Similarly, for
	</p>

	<Equation
	  tex={String.raw`\phi : \R \rightarrow \left( 0, \infty \right), \phi(x) = e^x; \phi^{-1}(x) = \ln x,`}
	/>

	<p> we get </p>

	<Equation
	  tex={String.raw`(\ln t, \ln^2 t).`}
	/>

	<p> If we wanted to be more esoteric than that, we could even define </p>

	<Equation
          tex={String.raw`\phi : \R \rightarrow \left( -1, 1 \right), \phi(x) = \tanh x = \frac{e^x - e^{-x}}{e^x + e^{-x}} = \frac{e^{2x} - 1}{e^{2x} + 1}.`}
        />

	<p>
	  This is what's known as the <span class="emph">hyperbolic tangent</span>. You can check out more on
          the <span class="emph">trigonometric hyperbolic functions</span> by clicking <span class="emph">here</span>, but here's a rough argument for
	  why <Math tex = "\tanh"/> is bijective. In analogous fashion to standard trigonometric functions,
	</p>

	<Equation
	  tex={String.raw`(\tanh x)' = \text{sech}^2 x > 0 \forall x \in \R,`}
	/>

	<p> meaning <Math tex = "\tanh"/> is strictly increasing, hence injective. Since, in addition to that, it is clearly continuous and </p>

	<Equation
	  tex={String.raw`\lim_{x\rightarrow -\infty}\tanh x = -1, \lim_{x\rightarrow\infty}\tanh x = 1,`}
	/>

	<p>
	  the range of <Math tex = "\tanh"/> is therefore <Math tex = "(-1, 1)"/>, which implies <Math tex = "\tanh"/> is also surjective. By equating
	</p>

	<Equation
	  tex={String.raw`\frac{e^{2x} - 1}{e^{2x} + 1} = t \Rightarrow t = \frac{1}{2}\ln\left( \frac{1 + t}{1 - t} \right) = \text{arctanh } t.`}
	/>

	<p> We arrived at yet another parametric form: </p>

	<Equation
	  tex={String.raw`\left( \frac{1}{2}\ln\left( \frac{1 + t}{1 - t} \right), \frac{1}{4}\ln^2\left( \frac{1 + t}{1 - t} \right) \right).`}
	/>

	<p> Who doesn't love needlessly complicated formulas for describing substrantially simpler notions? Yay. We can be even more up our own arses and define </p>

	<Equation
	  tex={String.raw`\phi : \R \rightarrow \R, \phi(x) = x + x^3.`}
	/>

	Since

	<Equation
	  tex={String.raw`(x + x^3)' = 1 + 3x^2 > 0 \forall x \in \R; \lim_{x\rightarrow\pm\infty} = \pm\infty,`}
	/>

	<p> this is another valid change of parameter, and using <span class="emph">Cardano's formula</span> to compute its inverse, we end up with </p>

	<Equation
	  tex={String.raw`\left(
          \sqrt[3]{\frac{t}{2} + \sqrt{\frac{t^2}{4} + \frac{1}{27}}} + \sqrt[3]{\frac{t}{2} - \sqrt{\frac{t^2}{4} + \frac{1}{27}}},
	  \left(\sqrt[3]{\frac{t}{2} + \sqrt{\frac{t^2}{4} + \frac{1}{27}}} + \sqrt[3]{\frac{t}{2} - \sqrt{\frac{t^2}{4} + \frac{1}{27}}}\right)^2
          \right).`}
	/>

      </section>

